```apex
public with sharing class CaseSurveyJob implements Queueable {

    private final List<Id> caseIds;

    // Convenience constructor for a single case (e.g. if something else
    // wants to enqueue a survey job for just one case).
    public CaseSurveyJob(Id caseId) {
        this.caseIds = new List<Id>{ caseId };
    }

    public CaseSurveyJob(List<Id> caseIds) {
        this.caseIds = caseIds;
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        if (caseIds == null || caseIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Process the first case in this execution. Each case is handled
        // independently - if creating its Task fails for any reason, we
        // swallow the error (logging it) so that it cannot prevent the
        // remaining cases in the chain from being processed.
        Id currentCaseId = caseIds[0];
        List<Id> remaining = new List<Id>(caseIds);
        remaining.remove(0);

        try {
            processCase(currentCaseId);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                'CaseSurveyJob failed for Case ' + currentCaseId + ': ' + e.getMessage());
        }

        // Chain to the next case, if any. Chaining from within a running
        // Queueable is allowed even when the 50-jobs-per-transaction
        // synchronous limit has already been reached, so this safely
        // handles arbitrarily large batches (e.g. 200 cases from the
        // nightly cleanup).
        if (!remaining.isEmpty() && !Test.isRunningTest()) {
            System.enqueueJob(new CaseSurveyJob(remaining));
        } else if (!remaining.isEmpty() && Test.isRunningTest()) {
            // In tests, chained async jobs can still be enqueued as long
            // as we haven't already exceeded the test's own queueable
            // limit; Test.stopTest() will run all chained jobs to
            // completion, so we still enqueue here.
            System.enqueueJob(new CaseSurveyJob(remaining));
        }
    }

    private void processCase(Id caseId) {
        Case c = [
            SELECT Id, CaseNumber, ContactId
            FROM Case
            WHERE Id = :caseId
            LIMIT 1
        ];

        // Defensive re-check: the contact could theoretically have been
        // cleared between enqueue and execution.
        if (c.ContactId == null) {
            return;
        }

        Task t = new Task(
            WhatId = c.Id,
            WhoId = c.ContactId,
            Subject = 'CSAT survey: ' + c.CaseNumber,
            ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(2),
            Status = 'Not Started'
        );

        insert t;
    }
}
```

**What changed and why**

The previous version enqueued one `CaseSurveyJob` per case directly from the trigger. That works fine for small batches, but Salesforce only allows **50** `System.enqueueJob` calls from a *synchronous* context (like a trigger) per transaction. The nightly cleanup closes up to 200 cases in one transaction, so the 51st `enqueueJob` call threw `System.LimitException: Too many queueable jobs added to the queue: 51`.

The fix uses **queueable chaining** instead of one-enqueue-per-case-from-the-trigger:

1. The trigger collects all newly-closed case Ids (with a contact) into a single list and makes **one** `enqueueJob` call, passing the whole list to `CaseSurveyJob`. This is always within the 50-job synchronous limit no matter how many cases are closed in one transaction.
2. `CaseSurveyJob.execute` processes **one case at a time**: it pops the first Id off the list, creates the Task for that case (wrapped in try/catch so a failure for one case is logged and doesn't stop processing of the rest), and then — if there are remaining Ids — enqueues a new `CaseSurveyJob` with the rest of the list.
3. Chaining (`enqueueJob` called from inside a running Queueable) is allowed by Salesforce even after the 50-job synchronous limit has been reached; the only restriction is that you can start just 1 new job per already-running job, which is exactly what this code does. This lets all 200 cases be processed, one job execution per case, without hitting any governor limit, and preserves the key requirement that a failure processing one case cannot affect the others.
4. `Test.isRunningTest()` branches are harmless no-ops here (both branches do the same enqueue); they're left in only for clarity/future tuning, but functionally the chain always continues when there's remaining work, and `Test.startTest()/stopTest()` in the test class will flush the entire chain to completion.

